{% block title %}Plugins{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<h1>Plugins</h1>

<p>Mel is fully plugin-driven. Each repo has a local <code>.mel/</code> directory containing installed plugins and configuration.</p>

<h2>Structure</h2>
<pre><code>
.mel/
  config.json                 # Repo-local user config (merged on top)
  plugins/
    &lt;plugin-name&gt;/
      config.json             # Plugin config (scripts, hooks, config)
      bin/                    # Executable scripts (optional)
</code></pre>

<h2>Installing</h2>
<pre><code>mel plugin install git
mel plugin install hg
mel plugin install mel-docs
</code></pre>

<p>Mel first looks for built-in defaults under <code>plugin_defaults/&lt;name&gt;</code> (shipped with the CLI). If not found, Mel falls back to generating minimal scaffolding. You can also ship plugins in your repo by committing <code>.mel/plugins/&lt;name&gt;</code>.</p>

<h2>Plugin Config Schema</h2>
<pre><code>{
  "scripts": {
    "command_name": {
      "description": "Short help text",
      "help_text": "Long-form documentation (optional)",
      "usage": "mel command [args]",
      "mode": "basic|advanced|both|none",
      "commands": [ "bash or shell command", "another" ],
      "parameters": { "name": { "type": "string|integer|boolean", "required": true } }
    },
    "simple": "echo short form is a string command"
  },
  "hooks": {
    "pre_command": [".mel/plugins/foo/hooks/pre_command"],
    "post_command": [],
    "on_error": []
  },
  "config": { "setting_name": { "type": "string", "required": false } }
}
</code></pre>

<h2>Modes and Help Filtering</h2>
<p>Commands can be hidden or shown based on <code>mode</code>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>basic</code>: Shows in basic only</li>
  <li><code>advanced</code>: Shows in advanced only</li>
  <li><code>both</code>: Shows in both</li>
  <li><code>none</code>: Hidden from help</li>
  <li>String commands (short form) default to <code>both</code></li>
  <li>Set mode: <code>mel mode basic</code> or <code>mel mode advanced</code></li>
  <li>Docs plugin is advanced; it appears only in advanced mode</li>
  </ul>

<h2>Defaults and Templates</h2>
<p>On first run, if the repo contains <code>.mel/config_template.json</code>, Mel installs any plugins listed and merges <code>scripts</code>, <code>hooks</code>, and <code>config</code> into <code>.mel/config.json</code> (user overrides win). You can re-apply a template later:</p>
<pre><code>mel template:apply</code></pre>

<h2>Authoring Tips</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Prefer small shell commands in <code>commands</code>. For complex flows, call scripts in <code>bin/</code>.</li>
  <li>Keep parameters documented in the plugin config; they’re used for help.</li>
  <li>Ship your plugin via repo (<code>.mel/plugins/&lt;name&gt;</code>) or as part of CLI defaults (<code>plugin_defaults/&lt;name&gt;</code>).</li>
</ul>

